# SYNOPSIS

```usage
ns
```

# DESCRIPTION

`ns` prints the namespace it was started in, which is its parent's, as namespace(6) text: a script of `mount` and `bind` lines that rebuilds it.
There is a line for each member of each union, in the order the members were added, since a later one may need a path an earlier one made.

A mounted member is printed as `mount`, its server's name, the mount point and, if it has one, the attach name; a bound one as `bind`, the path bound and the mount point.
The first member of a union to be added has no flags; each later one has `-b` if it comes before every member added so far and `-a` otherwise; and a member that takes creates has `-c` as well.
A word holding white space, a quote, `$` or `#`, or an empty one, is quoted as rc(1) quotes it.

`ns` takes no arguments; any it is given are ignored.

# EXAMPLES

After two mounts of a host's file server:

```rc
% ns | tail -2
mount tcp!10.0.2.100!5640 /n/host
mount tcp!10.0.2.100!5640 /n/again
```

# SEE ALSO

namespace(6), ps(1), rc(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

`ns` exits `no namespace` when it was given none, and `too long`, printing nothing, when the text does not fit 8 KiB.

# BUGS

`ns` prints only its own namespace; it cannot print another process's, which `/proc/<pid>/ns` holds.

An empty namespace also exits `too long`.
